{"id": "AEC-R-threshold-disclosure", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "aec-scope", "epistemic": "provisional", "text": "A check finding whose verdict turns on a threshold shows the threshold and the measured value beside the rule and level ([threshold: ...; measured: ...]): observed and prospective tiers, outcome sufficiency, superseded references. Findings that turn on no threshold show none. Scope from handoff HO-AEC-20261105-A17J item 3 (prior-framework alignment adopted by the owner; private ledger, cited as text)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-P0@3"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.records.Finding.line"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.records._tier_findings"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.records._evidence_kind_findings"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.records._superseded_reference_findings"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-threshold-disclosure-tests", "version": 1, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "text": "tests/test_threshold_disclosure.py: 5 tests; each of the four threshold-governed findings shows its threshold and measured value; findings without a threshold (unknown type, dangling reference) show none. Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-threshold-disclosure@1"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-authorship", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "aec-scope", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "Records can say who wrote them (authored_by: an actor id) and how that author stands to the code they describe (author_role: owner, independent or implementer), so a Dual-Channel Coding test can compare drift caught by independently written records against records written by the implementer. A field, not a workflow: check validates only the shape. Scope from handoff HO-AEC-20261105-A17J item 5 (prior-framework alignment and Dual-Channel Coding reactivation adopted by the owner; private ledger, cited as text)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-P0@3"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.records._authorship_findings"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-authorship-tests", "version": 1, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_authorship.py, written failing before the implementation: an unknown role, a role without an author, and a non-string or empty author are errors; an odd actor spelling is a warning; no fields, and five valid actor/role pairs, are clean. Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-authorship@1"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R1", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "aec-purpose", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "Never create a competing authority: Spec Kit owns workflow; Telosmith owns purpose, authority-by-subject, evidence status and criteria versions. Telosmith may regenerate fenced, hash-stamped derived blocks in specs from records by an explicit command (never a hook), and never edits authored text", "rationale": "@2: DEC-194 asks that specs be derived from Telosmith records; DEC-201 (Zach: \"Yes to 1 and 2\") amends constitution Principle I accordingly. Derived blocks are views of the records, so no second authority is created.", "quote": "Yes to 1 and 2. Work with everyone to figure out.", "origin": "Zach, Telosmith lane session 01GZK2Kne8fYjfQJz1kDge9, 2026-11-10 17:39 CT", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R1@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-P0@3"}], "anchors": [{"path": "governance/decisions.jsonl", "sha256": "e23a973257cdfe9e56c52ae90c71956dd54dc80267ba1b3b2ba8c7ad81920f30", "locator": "q:\"id\": \"DEC-201\"", "for": "rationale", "root": "relay-kit-private", "prefix_bytes": 230803}]}
{"id": "DEC-200", "version": 1, "type": "decision", "subject": "aec-scope", "status": "accepted", "decided_by": "AU-zach", "text": "Build gate is an asserted benefit (a plausible, explainable improvement with its mechanism stated), not a demonstrated one; nothing pointless is built except as a labelled experiment or exploration, which are first-class; work may ship live before its benefit is demonstrated; public value claims stay evidence-bound; the DEC-021 comparison feeds on live use and does not gate (projection of relay-kit-private DEC-200; constitution 2.0.0 Principles IV and V)", "quote": "There has to be plausible, explainable improvements. We have to be thinking that something will happen. ... But it should have an asserted benefit. Maybe we don't claim that, but that can stay as part of the gate.", "origin": "Zach, Telosmith lane session 01GZK2Kne8fYjfQJz1kDge9, 2026-11-10 17:39 CT", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-R3@3"}], "anchors": [{"path": "governance/decisions.jsonl", "sha256": "e23a973257cdfe9e56c52ae90c71956dd54dc80267ba1b3b2ba8c7ad81920f30", "locator": "q:\"id\": \"DEC-200\"", "for": "rationale", "root": "relay-kit-private", "prefix_bytes": 230803}]}
{"id": "DEC-201", "version": 1, "type": "decision", "subject": "aec-scope", "status": "accepted", "decided_by": "AU-zach", "text": "Constitution Principle I amended (2.0.0) so Telosmith may regenerate fenced, hash-stamped derived blocks in specs by an explicit command, never touching authored text (AEC-R1@2); telosmith speckit derive ships live as an experiment on one or two real repos (projection of relay-kit-private DEC-201)", "quote": "Yes to 1 and 2. Work with everyone to figure out.", "origin": "Zach, Telosmith lane session 01GZK2Kne8fYjfQJz1kDge9, 2026-11-10 17:39 CT", "relations": [{"rel": "depends_on", "to": "AEC-R1@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "governance/decisions.jsonl", "sha256": "e23a973257cdfe9e56c52ae90c71956dd54dc80267ba1b3b2ba8c7ad81920f30", "locator": "q:\"id\": \"DEC-201\"", "for": "rationale", "root": "relay-kit-private", "prefix_bytes": 230803}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:kairos-coord", "text": "The Claude hook and CI snippet that aec init generates run every aec call as python3 -P (Python 3.11+, older refused), so a module placed in the checkout can never shadow the installed Telosmith.", "source": "Codex security review (P1) on eraevous/crank-mem-eval#7, 2026-11-11; Zach 16:45 CT approved the rollout", "rationale": "The hook cd-s into the checkout before running Python, which puts the checkout first on the import path.", "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "6645e92a009cc71e9fcfd4b731d643b1db6cd52d2a9509dc39f3df0b939fbbab", "locator": "q:HOOK_COMMAND = (", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:kairos-coord", "text": "The Claude hook that aec init generates runs every aec call through a shell function that removes the checkout from sys.path before running the module, on every supported Python (3.10+), so a module placed in the checkout can never shadow the installed Telosmith. CI snippets are out of scope: a pull request can edit the workflow itself.", "source": "Codex security review (P1) on eraevous/crank-mem-eval#7, 2026-11-11; v1 used python3 -P, which Python 3.10 lacks (CI test (3.10) failed)", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "0dc6811f6cb6f67555579056d812fcdc59be6dd0ff0109061aafd332ee85102f", "locator": "q:HOOK_AEC = (", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path", "version": 3, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:kairos-coord", "text": "The Claude hook that aec init generates starts Python from / (aec() { ( cd / && python3 -m aec \"$@\" ); }) and passes the project as absolute paths, so no module in the checkout is importable from interpreter startup on, on every supported Python. If refreshing edges fails, the hook deletes the stale edges file and skips the records check rather than validating against old edges. CI snippets are out of scope: a pull request can edit the workflow itself.", "source": "Codex security review (P1) on eraevous/crank-mem-eval#7 at d1d230c: v2's launcher imported runpy before filtering sys.path; Copilot on the same PR: an ignored edges failure left stale edges in use", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "0f1dd17e1db1ee6ff97895ff3c48d16ee595a86a218f9ac9e3a139d0040dfa27", "locator": "q:HOOK_AEC =", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path", "version": 4, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "The Claude hook that aec init generates starts Python from / and passes the project as absolute paths, so no module in the checkout is importable from interpreter startup on, on every supported Python. Inside the launcher's subshell, PYTHONPATH loses every entry that resolves (after symlinks) inside the checkout, filtered by an isolated interpreter (python3 -I) also started from /, and a relative RELAYKIT_RECORDS is anchored to the project directory, which is what it meant when the hook ran inside the checkout. If refreshing edges fails, the hook deletes the stale edges file and skips the records check rather than validating against old edges. CI snippets are out of scope: a pull request can edit the workflow itself.", "source": "Copilot review on eraevous/Telosmith#77 at 637782f: an inherited absolute PYTHONPATH entry into the checkout still imported a branch aec.py; cd / turned RELAYKIT_RECORDS=records into /records", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path@3"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "af2bce107982224004be9aabe45494d4699e46497c607d1a614111eb4e4c1076", "locator": "q:HOOK_AEC =", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-speckit-derive", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "aec-scope", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "Spec Kit spec blocks can be derived from records: telosmith speckit derive regenerates fenced, hash-stamped blocks in spec.md and the constitution from requirement, criterion, purpose and implemented_by records, never touching authored text and never overwriting a block edited by hand; telosmith speckit drift reports spec/record mismatches in both directions and proposes provisional records without appending them. Optional: nothing requires Spec Kit. Scope from handoff HO-AEC-20261110-DW5N (owner direction DEC-194, private ledger, cited as text); acceptance is the owner's", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-P0@3"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.derive_text"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.drift_text"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.uncovered"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests", "version": 1, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_speckit_derive.py: 16 tests on a synthetic repo. Authored text survives regeneration; regeneration is idempotent, including on a CRLF checkout; a hand-edited block is left alone (violation) while an unedited one regenerates (decoy); drift finds unrecorded FR lines, unresolved and superseded citations, stale and edited blocks, and committed requirements no spec covers, each with a clean decoy; drift writes nothing.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@1"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-speckit-derive", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "aec-scope", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "Spec Kit spec blocks can be derived from records: telosmith speckit derive regenerates fenced, hash-stamped blocks in spec.md and the constitution from requirement, purpose and implemented_by records, showing each record at its baseline (newest non-provisional version) with newer provisional versions listed as pending, never touching authored text and never overwriting a block edited by hand; telosmith speckit drift reports spec/record mismatches in both directions and proposes provisional records without appending them. Acceptance criteria are not derived until a criterion-to-requirement relation exists. Optional: nothing requires Spec Kit. Scope from handoff HO-AEC-20261110-DW5N (owner direction DEC-194, DEC-201; private ledger, cited as text); acceptance is the owner's", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-P0@3"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@1"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.derive_text"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.drift_text"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.uncovered"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.speckit.baseline"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests", "version": 2, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_speckit_derive.py: 18 tests on a synthetic repo. Adds to v1: a provisional version appended over a committed one does not displace the committed baseline in the block, the constitution or coverage, and is not reported as superseding it (violation); deleting an edited block and running derive --insert restores it (recovery path); acceptance is no longer derived from the tests relation. Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests@1"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests", "version": 3, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_speckit_derive.py: 19 tests. Adds to v2: record text containing the closing-fence marker no longer closes a derived block early (failed before the fix: the untouched block was reported as hand-edited and skipped); the end fence matches only a standalone line and fence markers in record text are escaped. Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests@2"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests", "version": 4, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_speckit_derive.py: 21 tests. Adds to v3, each failing before its fix: derive writes through a unique, exclusively created temp file, so an existing spec.md.telosmith-tmp symlink's target is untouched and the spec keeps its permissions; an unloadable record (bad version) stops derive and drift with exit 2 instead of working from a partial ledger; FR ids with a letter suffix (FR-005a) are checked. Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests@3"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests", "version": 5, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_speckit_derive.py: 24 tests. Adds to v4, each failing before its fix: the constitution block lists newer provisional purpose and requirement versions as pending (so drift reports the block stale) while keeping the committed baseline; two different lines for one id@version stop derive and drift with exit 2; a byte-identical duplicate line is still accepted (decoy). Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests@4"}]}
{"id": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests", "version": 6, "type": "evidence", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "tests/test_speckit_derive.py: 26 tests. Adds to v5, each failing before its fix: derive refuses (exit 2, nothing written) when the spec or constitution target is the records file, directly, through a symlink or through a hard link; drift proposals consult the records, so unchanged text yields no proposal (cite the existing record) and revised text yields the next version superseding the latest, never a reused id@1. Full suite passes.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "AEC-R-speckit-derive@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "EV-R-speckit-derive-tests@5"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-user-hook", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "Draft for DEC-223 (design proposed, not accepted): `aec hook install` writes a user-level Claude Code PostToolUse hook that runs the interpreter of the environment aec was installed into, by absolute path, with -I and from /, only in repositories that have .aec/records.jsonl. Before running it verifies that aec was imported from inside that environment, that every file of the aec package is listed in pip's RECORD with a matching sha256, and that RECORD matches the digest pinned at install; otherwise it refuses with the reason (exit 2). It adds a check and does not stop a checkout's own project hooks, because Claude Code merges hooks across settings levels.", "source": "HO-AEC-20261112-NBRW (relay-kit-private), DEC-223: Zach 2026-11-12 08:29 CT 'I approve of the user-level hooks.'; integrity layer from Zach 08:32 CT 'Hash it first, compare, reject if broken?'", "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/userhook.py", "sha256": "d02697da544308ec34af4fa38ff9c4bee63161b2fdd71044647e0d324215a20d", "locator": "q:def hook_command", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-user-hook", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "Draft for DEC-223 (design proposed, not accepted): `aec hook install` writes a user-level Claude Code PostToolUse hook that runs the interpreter of the environment aec was installed into, by absolute path, with -I and from /, only in repositories that have .aec/records.jsonl. The hook verifies BEFORE importing any aec module, with stdlib-only bootstrap source passed via -c: the telosmith distribution's aec package lies inside a dedicated environment, every package file is listed in pip's RECORD with a matching sha256, and RECORD matches the digest pinned at install; otherwise it refuses with the reason (exit 2). Install and uninstall touch only the marked command, keeping sibling hooks in the same entry. It adds a check and does not stop a checkout's own project hooks, because Claude Code merges hooks across settings levels.", "source": "Codex review on eraevous/Telosmith#79 at c325dc3 (P1: package modules imported by aec.__main__ ran before the in-package check; P2: uninstall dropped sibling hooks in a shared entry); HO-AEC-20261112-NBRW; DEC-223", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-user-hook@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/userhook.py", "sha256": "eb2de03c7ddc60aa8edc5d550729af88ba96d1d965e738018962d433b7cd01ce", "locator": "q:BOOTSTRAP_SRC = VERIFY_SRC", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-user-hook", "version": 3, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "Draft for DEC-223 (design proposed, not accepted): `aec hook install` writes a user-level Claude Code PostToolUse hook that runs the interpreter of the environment aec was installed into, by absolute path, with -I and from /, only in repositories that have .aec/records.jsonl. The hook verifies BEFORE importing any aec module, with stdlib-only bootstrap source passed via -c: the telosmith distribution's aec package lies inside a dedicated environment, every package file is listed in pip's RECORD with a matching sha256, and RECORD matches the digest pinned at install; otherwise it refuses with the reason (exit 2). It then imports aec with a private empty pycache_prefix and bytecode writes off, so only verified source runs, never a cached .pyc. Install and uninstall touch only the marked command, keeping sibling hooks in the same entry. It adds a check and does not stop a checkout's own project hooks, because Claude Code merges hooks across settings levels.", "source": "Codex review on eraevous/Telosmith#79 at 1de4f08 (P1: a tampered __pycache__ .pyc with a matching header bypassed RECORD and the pin); earlier findings at c325dc3; HO-AEC-20261112-NBRW; DEC-223", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-user-hook@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/userhook.py", "sha256": "4252aff5d9263d70a096ea5b08458edea87330749e9626a18ad12587e73f714f", "locator": "q:sys.pycache_prefix = tempfile.mkdtemp", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-user-hook", "version": 4, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "Draft for DEC-223 (design proposed, not accepted): `aec hook install` writes a user-level Claude Code PostToolUse hook that runs the interpreter of the environment aec was installed into, by absolute path, with -I and from /, only in repositories that have .aec/records.jsonl. The hook verifies BEFORE importing any aec module, with stdlib-only bootstrap source passed via -c: the telosmith distribution's aec package lies inside a dedicated environment, contains no symlinks, every package file is listed in pip's RECORD with a matching sha256, and RECORD matches the digest pinned at install; otherwise it refuses with the reason (exit 2). It then imports aec with a private empty pycache_prefix and bytecode writes off, so only verified source runs, never a cached .pyc. Install and uninstall touch only the marked command, keeping sibling hooks in the same entry. It adds a check and does not stop a checkout's own project hooks, because Claude Code merges hooks across settings levels.", "source": "Codex review on eraevous/Telosmith#79 at 52db2ad (P1: a symlinked directory inside the package escaped os.walk but was imported); earlier findings at c325dc3 and 1de4f08; HO-AEC-20261112-NBRW; DEC-223", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-user-hook@3"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/userhook.py", "sha256": "718c40de64bf1fa3a1391085b1d71b895a9149556d6ae5d803350b3df43b1adf", "locator": "q:is a symlink; the installed package must contain only regular files", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-user-hook", "version": 5, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "Draft for DEC-223 (design proposed, not accepted): `aec hook install` writes a user-level Claude Code PostToolUse hook that runs the interpreter of the environment aec was installed into, by absolute path, with -I and from /, only in repositories that have .aec/records.jsonl. The hook verifies BEFORE importing any aec module, with stdlib-only bootstrap source passed via -c: the telosmith distribution's aec package lies inside a dedicated environment, contains no symlinks, every package file is listed in pip's RECORD with a matching sha256, and RECORD matches the digest pinned at install; otherwise it refuses with the reason (exit 2). It then imports aec with a private empty pycache_prefix and bytecode writes off, so only verified source runs. It refuses a symlinked .aec directory or records file, and writes each output to a new file inside .aec renamed onto the destination, so a symlink there is replaced, never followed. Install and uninstall touch only the marked command, keeping sibling hooks in the same entry. It adds a check and does not stop a checkout's own project hooks, because Claude Code merges hooks across settings levels.", "source": "Codex security review on eraevous/Telosmith#79 at 0647052 (P1: a symlinked .aec/edges.jsonl made the hook overwrite a user file); earlier findings at c325dc3, 1de4f08 and 52db2ad; HO-AEC-20261112-NBRW; DEC-223", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-user-hook@4"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/userhook.py", "sha256": "2b575e0af0bc66a83c9a2d00db4d682ddcbd9971a438a6b659a2eb10bec0576a", "locator": "q:def _produce(", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-user-hook", "version": 6, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "Draft for DEC-223 (design proposed, not accepted): `aec hook install` writes a user-level Claude Code PostToolUse hook that runs the interpreter of the environment aec was installed into, by absolute path, with -I and from /, only in repositories that have .aec/records.jsonl. The hook verifies BEFORE importing any aec module, with stdlib-only bootstrap source passed via -c: the telosmith distribution's aec package lies inside a dedicated environment, contains no symlinks, every package file is listed in pip's RECORD with a matching sha256, every hashed RECORD file in the package still exists (read from RECORD itself), the top-level aec import resolves to the verified __init__.py, and RECORD matches the digest pinned at install; otherwise it refuses with the reason (exit 2). It then imports aec with a private empty pycache_prefix and bytecode writes off, so only verified source runs. It refuses a symlinked .aec directory or records file, and writes each output to a new file inside .aec renamed onto the destination. Install and uninstall touch only the marked command. It adds a check and does not stop a checkout's own project hooks, because Claude Code merges hooks across settings levels.", "source": "Codex security review on eraevous/Telosmith#79 at 7ecc80d (P1: a deleted aec/__init__.py plus an aec.py in site-packages ran unverified code); earlier findings at c325dc3, 1de4f08, 52db2ad and 0647052; HO-AEC-20261112-NBRW; DEC-223", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-user-hook@5"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/userhook.py", "sha256": "109897be19522f2a221eec1739aa3e6f550724f080bb13804641607cfa574164", "locator": "q:is listed in RECORD but missing", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-symlink-outputs", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "aec edges -o, aec edges --legacy-csv and aec snapshot -o write a fresh file and rename it onto the destination, so a symlink planted there is replaced, never written through. The Claude hook that aec init generates refuses to run (exit 2) when .aec, .aec/records.jsonl, .aec/edges.jsonl or .aec/hook-before.jsonl is a symlink, and aec init treats a dangling symlink as present rather than creating its target.", "source": "Codex security review on eraevous/Telosmith#79 (7ecc80d) found the user-level hook wrote through a planted symlink; the project hook had the same gap. Zach 2026-11-15 11:03 CT approved the narrow repair (decision sheet reading 2, \"2, yes\"; HO-KAI-20261115-D3ZR)", "rationale": "A branch controls everything under .aec; writing through a symlink there lets it overwrite any file the developer owns on the next edit.", "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/edges.py", "sha256": "cc6d4d235558d8d5dfd4adca33e9e621e95639bddde2de20113c848adb32ea0e", "locator": "q:def write_replacing", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path", "version": 5, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "The Claude hook that aec init generates starts Python from / and passes the project as absolute paths, so no module in the checkout is importable from interpreter startup on, on every supported Python. Inside the launcher's subshell, an isolated interpreter (python3 -I) also started from / rewrites PYTHONPATH: a relative entry is resolved against the project directory (what it meant when the hook ran inside the checkout) and passed on as an absolute path, absolute entries pass unchanged, and every entry that resolves (after symlinks) inside the checkout is dropped. A relative RELAYKIT_RECORDS is anchored to the project directory the same way. If refreshing edges fails, the hook deletes the stale edges file and skips the records check rather than validating against old edges. CI snippets are out of scope: a pull request can edit the workflow itself.", "source": "Codex reviews on eraevous/Fathom#49, Today#7 and MtG#46 (hook e019ae40): after cd /, a relative sibling entry such as PYTHONPATH=../Telosmith/src was read from / and the hook skipped every check", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path@4"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "ba100ea793d3fc9dedc487daa69b8e9417d1e4afb7924a8fe45c1aa44d32e811", "locator": "q:HOOK_PATHFILTER =", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path", "version": 6, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "text": "The Claude hook that aec init generates starts Python from / and passes the project as absolute paths, so no module in the checkout is importable from interpreter startup on, on every supported Python. Inside the launcher's subshell, an isolated interpreter (python3 -I) also started from / rewrites PYTHONPATH: a relative entry is resolved against the project directory (what it meant when the hook ran inside the checkout) and passed on as an absolute path, absolute entries pass unchanged, and every entry that resolves (after symlinks) inside the checkout, is an ancestor of the checkout (which would make the checkout importable under its own directory name), or resolves to a path containing the path separator is dropped. A relative RELAYKIT_RECORDS is anchored to the project directory the same way. If refreshing edges fails, the hook deletes the stale edges file and skips the records check rather than validating against old edges. CI snippets are out of scope: a pull request can edit the workflow itself.", "source": "Copilot review on eraevous/Telosmith#87 at 9375df6: a relative or absolute ancestor entry made a checkout named aec/ importable as aec; a symlinked src resolving to a path containing ':' smuggled a second entry inside the checkout", "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path@5"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "7b60c1c71950be7a8f8969e4051a82faf309b622ec4fef8bbf5da90e7e2461f1", "locator": "q:HOOK_PATHFILTER =", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-D-braided-dcc", "version": 1, "type": "decision", "subject": "aec-scope", "status": "proposed", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "Proposed: Kairos development uses Braided Dual-Channel Coding. Three actors per change: the lane implements; a separate semantic maintainer (default the lane's paired Kate, author_role independent; fallback Research or a fresh-context Claude) writes the purpose, requirement and decision records and checks code-to-record drift; Codex reviews. author_role independent is a declaration, not proof: transcribed records keep a source reference, the exact authored text and the revision shown; a maintainer without the diff, records and test evidence reports not assessed, never clean. Fields first: aec check reports counts of independent, implementer and unattributed records, lists self-maintained requirements as info, and warns only when a record claims independent while naming the same actor as its implementer; nothing blocks until the owner has seen a week of results. Propagation: Telosmith owns the hook body (aec hook run) and the records format so repos stop inlining hook logic, and no existing hook is replaced before pinned local resolution, version-mismatch diagnostics, compatibility tests and a rollback path exist; Lydian owns the CI that pins and runs Telosmith and the bot that proposes pin bumps. Pilot: the crank-mem rebase. Owner direction to roll out live, quoted by the coordinator in relay-kit-private HO-AEC-20261117-DBN5 (private ledger, cited as text); method from relay-kit-private DEC-099 item 3.", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "AEC-P0@3"}, {"rel": "depends_on", "to": "AEC-R-authorship@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/integrations/braided-dcc.md", "sha256": "ee52d010c1ddac953ff3dfc9d2d7665fdcf6708addb744e2b8d5d066065cfd9a", "locator": "q:## 1. What the method is", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-line", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "claude:aec", "author_role": "implementer", "text": "aec init --hook-style line writes a project hook whose settings text holds only the launcher (the same PYTHONPATH filter and start from / as the inline hook) and calls aec hook project, the hook body shipped in the package. On the same checkout it exits as the inline hook does: 0 when quiet, 1 when a step could not run, 2 with a report for the agent; a symlink at .aec, the records file or either output is refused. When no aec is importable, or the importable aec has no hook project, it says which and exits 1. inline stays the default until the line resolves the version CI pins (Kate gate on Telosmith#88). Out of scope: verifying the installed files (that is the user-level hook) and replacing any repository existing hook.", "source": "HO-AEC-20261117-DBN5, AEC-D-braided-dcc@1 propagation D; Kate review conditions on Telosmith#88 (kate-replies:replies/HO-AEC-20261117-DBN5--6e5627398c5a.md). Implementer-written; the independent records are asked of Kate.", "relations": [{"rel": "depends_on", "to": "AEC-D-braided-dcc@1"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.aec.userhook.project_run"}, {"rel": "depends_on", "to": "AEC-R-hook-safe-path@6"}], "anchors": [{"path": "src/aec/init.py", "sha256": "ed85a5ab20bddc35db46488ebf4c5e2e35fbcd11da5c0e1c7ccea25e6917407e", "locator": "q:HOOK_LINE_COMMAND =", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "AEC-R-hook-line-launcher", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "code", "epistemic": "provisional", "authored_by": "chatgpt:kate", "author_role": "independent", "text": "Requirement: `aec init --hook-style line` shall generate a launcher-only project hook that uses the inline launcher's PYTHONPATH filtering and starts from `/` to invoke the package-owned `aec hook project`, while omission of `--hook-style` retains inline generation.", "test_must_show": ["Default generation remains inline.", "Explicit line generation delegates the body to the package rather than embedding it.", "Both launchers apply equivalent filtering and startup-directory handling, including with checkout-local import-shadowing fixtures."], "out_of_scope": "changing the inline default, automatic migration, and package installation.", "source": "kate-replies:replies/HO-AEC-20261117-DBN5--0506d49d889c.md (API Kate, gpt-6.1-sol, 2026-11-18T01:22:18Z). Transcribed verbatim by claude:aec#wake-20261118-03: requirement into text, her 'Test must show' and 'Out of scope' bullets into test_must_show and out_of_scope; relations added by the transcriber.", "shown": "relay-kit-private 16dc713, handoffs/outputs/HO-AEC-20261117-DBN5.md (the round-2 ask and the wake summary only); not the PR diff, the tests or the text of AEC-R-hook-line@1. Implementation conformance: not assessed by the maintainer.", "relations": [{"rel": "depends_on", "to": "AEC-D-braided-dcc@1"}]}
